The original does a linear scan over every message definition on each call. This version builds a 64K-entry index table once and then answers each lookup with a single array access.

- **Same result as the scan:** the table is filled from the last definition to the first. So when a number appears more than once, the first match wins, as in the original.
- **Missing numbers:** they map to a sentinel value, so the function still returns `NULL` for them.
- **No dependence on mutable state:** the table is built only from the constant `mesgs` array.
- **Thread-safe setup:** it is built once through a function-local static, whose initialization C++11 and later guarantee to be thread-safe.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Index
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        Index()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < 65536; k++)
                idx[k] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
                idx[mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        }
    };
    static const Index index;

    const FIT_UINT16 i = index.idx[num];
    if (i == 0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[i];
}
```